/* ===== Swing trading D: strategies, stops & targets, risk, markets, psychology ===== */

STT('st-strategies', { name: 'Core swing-trading strategies', cat: 'ststrat',
  blurb: `Everything so far — trend, levels, patterns, indicators — comes together in a handful of repeatable strategies. Most swing profits come from a few setups traded consistently. This page is the menu; the entry, stop, target and risk pages make each one tradable.`,
  facts: [['Bread & butter', 'trend pullback'], ['High reward', 'breakout'], ['Range', 'support/resistance bounce'], ['Pick', '1–2 setups, master them']],
  cheat: [['Trend pullback', 'Buy dips to support/MA in an uptrend (sell rallies in a downtrend)'], ['Breakout', 'Enter as price breaks a level/pattern on volume'], ['Pullback-to-MA', 'Buy the bounce off a key moving average in a trend'], ['S/R bounce (range)', 'Buy support, sell resistance inside a range'], ['Reversal', 'Trade a confirmed top/bottom pattern at a level'], ['Momentum', 'Ride strong, high-RVOL moves with the trend'], ['Mean reversion', 'Fade overstretched moves back to average (ranges)'], ['Best odds', 'with the higher-timeframe trend'], ['Each needs', 'a level, a trigger, a stop, a target'], ['Specialise', 'master a couple, not all']],
  sections: [
    ['The core strategies, and when each works', TBL(['Strategy', 'Idea', 'Best market condition'], [['Trend pullback', 'Join a trend after a counter-move pauses at support/MA', 'Clear, healthy trend (the bread-and-butter swing trade)'], ['Breakout', 'Enter as price breaks a level/pattern with volume', 'Consolidation resolving; momentum; after a squeeze'], ['Pullback-to-MA', 'Buy the bounce off the 20/50 MA in a trend', 'Strong, orderly trend respecting its MA'], ['Range / S-R bounce', 'Buy support, sell resistance within a box', 'Sideways, range-bound market'], ['Reversal', 'Trade a confirmed reversal pattern at a major level', 'Trend exhaustion at a key S/R zone'], ['Mean reversion', 'Fade an overstretched move back to the mean', 'Range or quiet market, not strong trends']]) + PP(`The first rule across all of them: <b>match the strategy to the market condition</b>. Trend tactics in a range (or range tactics in a trend) is the most common way setups fail.`)],
    ['The bread-and-butter: trend pullback', FIG(chart({ w: 560, h: 250, candles: trend(20, 20, 0.22, 0.7, 3).concat(trend(6, 24.4, -0.25, 0.4, 8), trend(10, 23, 0.26, 0.6, 2)),
      tlines: [{ from: [1, 19.9], to: [34, 26.0], label: 'trend support' }], marks: [{ i: 24, p: 23.0, type: 'buy', label: 'buy the pullback' }] }),
      'Uptrend → price pulls back to the rising trend support / moving average → a bullish reversal candle triggers a buy, joining the next up-swing. This is the highest-frequency, most forgiving swing setup for beginners.')],
    ['Breakout vs pullback — two ways to enter a trend', PP(`<b>Breakout</b> trades enter as price pushes <i>through</i> a level or pattern (resistance, a triangle) on strong volume — you catch the move early but face more fakeouts, so volume confirmation and a retest option matter. <b>Pullback</b> trades enter <i>after</i> the trend is established, buying the dip to support/MA — a better price and tighter stop, but you may miss trends that never pull back. Many traders use both: breakouts to get in on fresh moves, pullbacks to add or enter established trends.`)],
    ['Choosing your setups', UL([`<b>Specialise:</b> pick <b>one or two</b> setups and trade them until you know every nuance — win rate, best conditions, typical failure. Breadth comes later.`, `<b>With the trend</b> first: trend pullbacks and with-trend breakouts are the most forgiving for beginners; reversals and mean-reversion are higher-skill.`, `<b>Define it fully:</b> each setup needs a <b>location</b> (level/MA), a <b>trigger</b> (candle/break), a <b>stop</b>, and a <b>target</b> — the next pages.`, `<b>Journal by setup</b> so you learn which ones actually make <i>you</i> money.`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Using a trend strategy in a range (or vice versa) is the top cause of failed setups — match tactic to condition.`, `Trying to trade every strategy at once means mastering none; specialise.`, `Counter-trend reversals and mean-reversion are higher-skill — beginners should lean on with-trend pullbacks/breakouts.`, `A "strategy" without defined stop and target rules is not a strategy — it is a hope.`, `Backtest/journal each setup separately; your edge may come from just one of them.`],
  faq: [['Which strategy should a beginner start with?', PP(`The <b>trend pullback</b>: in a clear uptrend, wait for a dip to support or a key moving average, and buy on a bullish reversal candle. It is the most forgiving because you are trading <i>with</i> the dominant force, you get a good price (buying the dip rather than chasing), and your stop is tight (just below the support/MA). Master this one setup — location, trigger, stop, target — before adding breakouts or reversals.`)],
    ['Breakout or pullback entries — which is better?', PP(`Neither is universally better; they fit different moments. <b>Breakouts</b> catch new moves early but have more fakeouts (so demand volume and consider a retest entry). <b>Pullbacks</b> give a better price and tighter stop but can miss runaway trends. A common approach: take with-trend <b>breakouts</b> to enter fresh momentum, and <b>pullbacks</b> to enter or add to trends that are already established. Pick based on the setup in front of you, and journal which works for you.`)],
    ['How many setups do I need to be profitable?', PP(`<b>One good one, traded well.</b> Profitability comes from executing a positive-expectancy setup consistently with sound risk management — not from collecting strategies. Many professionals trade a single core setup for years. Add a second only once the first is consistently profitable and journaled, and only if it suits <i>different</i> conditions (e.g. a range strategy to complement a trend one).`)]],
  qs: [SQ('What is the most forgiving starting strategy for a beginner?', ['Counter-trend reversals', 'Trend pullback (buying dips in an uptrend)', 'Mean reversion in trends', 'Scalping breakouts'], 1, 'Trading with the trend, buying dips to support/MA, gives good prices, tight stops and better odds.'),
    SQ('What is the top cause of a swing setup failing?', ['Using the wrong colour chart', 'Applying a trend strategy in a range (or a range strategy in a trend)', 'Not enough indicators', 'Trading large caps'], 1, 'Matching the strategy to the market condition is essential; mismatches cause most failures.')]
});
DP('st-strategies', {
  fu: [['What is a “setup” vs an “entry”?', 'A setup is the full condition that makes a trade worth taking (e.g. uptrend + pullback to the 20-EMA at support). The entry is the precise trigger and price at which you act (e.g. a break of the reversal candle’s high). A setup can be present without a valid entry trigger yet.'],
    ['Why specialise instead of diversifying strategies?', 'Each strategy has a personality — best conditions, typical failures, ideal stops. Mastery (knowing when it works and when to skip it) comes from repetition and journaling one setup, which compounds faster than shallow familiarity with many.'],
    ['How do I know which condition the market is in?', 'Read structure on the daily/weekly: clean higher highs/lows = trend (use pullbacks/breakouts); flat, overlapping swings between levels = range (use S/R bounces/mean reversion); erratic, large opposing candles = chop (often best to stand aside).']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Classify the market first (trend/range/chop), then apply the matching setup with a defined location, trigger, stop and target. Journal each setup’s results separately to find your real edge.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Strategy–condition mismatch, trading too many setups shallowly, or running a "strategy" with no stop/target rules.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Most consistent swing traders run one or two well-defined setups with strict risk control; the edge is in selectivity and execution, not the number of strategies.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Swing Trading Strategies', 'https://www.investopedia.com/articles/trading/05/swingtrading.asp'] });

STT('st-entries', { name: 'Entries & triggers', cat: 'ststrat',
  blurb: `A setup tells you where to look; a trigger tells you when to pull the trigger. Good entries wait for confirmation at a level, keep the stop tight, and never chase. This page covers break vs retest vs candle triggers, and the discipline of waiting.`,
  facts: [['Need', 'a precise trigger, not a feeling'], ['Triggers', 'break, retest, reversal candle'], ['Chasing', 'the enemy of good entries'], ['Tight stop', 'the point of a good entry']],
  cheat: [['Confirmation entry', 'Wait for the trigger (safer, slightly later)'], ['Anticipation entry', 'Enter before confirmation (better price, riskier)'], ['Break entry', 'Enter on a decisive close through a level'], ['Retest entry', 'Enter when price returns to the broken level and holds'], ['Candle trigger', 'Enter on a reversal candle at a level'], ['Stop-order entry', 'Auto-enter on a breakout above the level'], ['Limit-order entry', 'Auto-enter at a planned pullback level'], ['Scaling in', 'Enter in parts to average into a zone'], ['Never chase', 'If you missed it, wait for the next setup'], ['Tight stop', 'Good entries put the stop close by']],
  sections: [
    ['Three ways to trigger an entry', TBL(['Trigger', 'How', 'Trade-off'], [['Reversal candle', 'Enter on/after a confirming candle at a level', 'Reliable; a little late'], ['Level break', 'Enter on a decisive close through resistance/pattern', 'Early; more fakeouts'], ['Break-and-retest', 'Enter when price returns to the broken level and holds', 'Best risk control; may miss no-retest moves']]) + PP(`All three share the same goal: get in <b>close to the point of invalidation</b> so the stop is tight and the reward-to-risk is large. A good entry is defined by where its <b>stop</b> can go, not by how clever it feels.`)],
    ['Break-and-retest, drawn', FIG(chart({ w: 560, h: 250, candles: trend(16, 20, 0.08, 0.6, 3).concat(trend(4, 21, 0.5, 0.5, 9), trend(4, 23, -0.35, 0.3, 2), trend(8, 22.1, 0.3, 0.5, 6)),
      hlines: [{ p: 21.9, label: 'broken resistance → support', cls: 'stc-sup' }], marks: [{ i: 25, p: 22.1, type: 'buy', label: 'retest entry' }] }),
      'Price breaks resistance, then pulls back to retest it as new support and holds. Entering on the retest gives a tight stop (just below the level) and confirmation that the breakout was real.')],
    ['Confirmation vs anticipation', PP(`<b>Confirmation</b> entries wait for the trigger to complete (the candle to close, the retest to hold) — higher probability, slightly worse price. <b>Anticipation</b> entries get in <i>before</i> confirmation (e.g. buying into support expecting a bounce) — better price and reward-to-risk, but a higher failure rate and the discipline cost of being early. Beginners should favour <b>confirmation</b>; it trades a little edge for far fewer bad trades and less second-guessing.`)],
    ['The discipline of not chasing', UL([`If price has already run from your planned entry, <b>let it go</b> — chasing means a worse price, a wider stop, and poor reward-to-risk.`, `Use <b>limit orders</b> at your planned pullback level and <b>stop orders</b> at your breakout level so the market comes to <i>you</i>; you do not have to watch every tick.`, `<b>Missing a trade costs nothing;</b> taking a bad-entry trade costs money. There is always another setup.`, `<b>Scaling in</b> (entering in parts across a zone) can reduce the pain of timing, but keep total risk fixed — more entries do not mean more risk.`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Chasing a move after it has run gives a bad price and a wide stop — wait for the next setup instead.`, `Anticipation entries (before confirmation) fail more often; beginners should wait for the trigger.`, `Breakout entries without a decisive close/volume are fakeout-prone — prefer the retest when unsure.`, `Entering far from your invalidation point makes the stop wide and reward-to-risk poor — good entries sit near the stop.`, `Scaling in must keep <i>total</i> risk constant; adding size to a loser ("averaging down") blows the risk plan.`],
  faq: [['Should I enter on the breakout or wait for the retest?', PP(`If you want the best <b>risk control</b> and can accept missing some moves, wait for the <b>retest</b>: price breaks the level, comes back to it, and holds (old resistance becomes support), letting you enter with a tight stop just below. If you prioritise not missing strong moves, take the <b>breakout</b> on a decisive close with volume — but accept more fakeouts. A common compromise: take a small position on the break and add on the retest.`)],
    ['What exactly is a good "trigger"?', PP(`A specific, observable event that confirms your setup and defines your stop — for example: a <b>bullish reversal candle</b> closing at support, a <b>decisive close</b> above a resistance level, or a <b>successful retest</b> of a broken level. A feeling ("it looks ready") is not a trigger. The trigger’s job is to get you in close to the point where the trade would be proven wrong, so your stop is tight.`)],
    ['Is it bad to miss a trade?', PP(`No — missing a trade costs you nothing but a hypothetical gain, while a bad entry costs real money and discipline. Markets offer endless setups; there is always another. The urge to chase a missed move (fear of missing out) leads to the worst entries. Set your orders in advance, let price come to your level, and if it does not, move on without regret.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('Which entry generally gives the best risk control?', ['Chasing after a big run', 'Break-and-retest (tight stop just below the reclaimed level)', 'Market-buying at any price', 'Entering on a feeling'], 1, 'The retest confirms the break and lets you place a tight stop, maximising reward-to-risk.'),
    SQ('You missed your planned entry and price has run. The disciplined action is to…', ['Chase with a market order', 'Wait for the next setup — missing a trade costs nothing', 'Double your size to catch up', 'Remove your stop and hope'], 1, 'Chasing gives a bad price and wide stop; there is always another setup.')]
});
DP('st-entries', {
  fu: [['What is scaling into a position?', 'Entering in two or more parts across a zone (e.g. half on the break, half on the retest, or in tranches into a support band) to reduce timing risk. Done right, total risk stays fixed — you size each tranche so the combined position still risks your set % if the stop hits.'],
    ['Why place orders in advance?', 'Pre-placing limit (pullback) and stop (breakout) orders removes the need to watch every tick and the temptation to chase or hesitate. The plan executes mechanically at your levels, which is exactly what swing trading’s days-to-weeks horizon allows.'],
    ['Does a tighter entry always mean a better trade?', 'A tighter entry (closer to the stop) improves reward-to-risk, but only if the trigger is valid — entering tight into an unconfirmed level just to shrink the stop raises the failure rate. Balance tightness with confirmation.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Define the trigger and the stop together: the best entries are where a valid confirmation sits right next to the invalidation level, giving a tight stop and large reward-to-risk. Pre-place orders.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Chasing, anticipating without confirmation, taking fakeout breakouts, or averaging down (adding to losers) and breaking the risk plan.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Break-and-retest and confirmed reversal-candle entries at levels are the staples; patience to wait for the trigger (and to skip missed trades) is what separates good entries from bad.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Entry and Exit Strategies', 'https://www.investopedia.com/articles/trading/08/trading-rules.asp'] });

STT('st-stops', { name: 'Stop loss: where and when', cat: 'ststrat',
  blurb: `The stop loss is the most important order you place — it caps the damage when you are wrong, and you will be wrong often. This page answers exactly where to put a stop (and where not to), when to set it (always, before entry), and how to size it to the market with ATR.`,
  facts: [['When', 'always, set before you enter'], ['Where', 'beyond the point that proves you wrong'], ['Size by', 'structure or ATR, not a round number'], ['Never', 'move it away from price']],
  cheat: [['Always use one', 'No stop = unlimited risk; non-negotiable'], ['Set before entry', 'Define the exit as part of the plan'], ['Structure stop', 'Just beyond the swing low/high or level'], ['ATR stop', 'Entry − (1.5–3 × ATR) for volatility room'], ['% stop', 'A fixed % (simple but ignores structure)'], ['Below the wick', 'Give the level room; avoid obvious stop-run prices'], ['Invalidation', 'The price where your idea is wrong'], ['Stop-market', 'Guarantees exit (may slip)'], ['Never widen', 'Moving a stop away from price = breaking the plan'], ['Trail to lock gains', 'Only move a stop toward price']],
  sections: [
    ['Where the stop goes: beyond invalidation', FIG(chart({ w: 560, h: 250, candles: [[24, 24.1, 23.0, 23.1], [23.1, 23.2, 22.1, 22.2], [22.15, 22.3, 21.3, 22.2], [22.2, 23.1, 22.1, 23.0], [23.0, 23.5, 22.8, 23.4]],
      hlines: [{ p: 22.1, label: 'support (invalidation zone)', cls: 'stc-sup' }],
      marks: [{ i: 3, p: 22.35, type: 'buy', label: 'entry' }, { i: 0, p: 21.15, type: 'stop', label: 'stop: below the wick, beyond support' }] }),
      'Place the stop just beyond the level/swing that would prove the idea wrong — below the support zone and the rejection wick, not right at the obvious level where stop-runs occur. If price trades there, buyers failed and you want out small.')],
    ['Three ways to place a stop', TBL(['Method', 'How', 'Pros / cons'], [['Structure-based', 'Just beyond the swing low/high or the level', 'Logical (exits when the idea is wrong); best default'], ['ATR-based', 'Entry − (1.5–3 × ATR)', 'Adapts to volatility; avoids random noise; great for sizing'], ['Percentage-based', 'A fixed % from entry (e.g. 5%)', 'Simple, but ignores where support/structure actually is']]) + PP(`The best stops combine them: place it at the <b>structure</b> (below support/swing low), and if that distance is unreasonably tight or wide for the instrument’s <b>ATR</b>, reconsider the trade or the entry. Then let <b>position size</b> — not a wider stop — control your dollar risk.`)],
    ['When to set the stop — and never widening it', PP(`Set the stop <b>before you enter</b>, as part of the plan, and ideally place it as a <b>stop-market order</b> immediately. The cardinal sin is <b>moving a stop away</b> from price "to give it room" when the trade goes against you — that turns a planned small loss into a large one and is how accounts blow up. A stop may only move <b>toward</b> price (to lock in profit or reduce risk), never away. If you find yourself wanting to widen a stop, the honest signal is that the trade is failing.`) + CO('Mindset:', 'A stop being hit is not a failure — it is the system working. The loss was defined and small. Being wrong cheaply is the whole point.')],
    ['Sizing the stop to the market with ATR', PP(`A fixed-distance stop ignores how much the instrument actually moves. <b>ATR</b> (from the indicators unit) measures the typical candle range, so a stop at <b>2×ATR</b> sits beyond normal noise and adapts automatically — wider in volatile conditions, tighter in calm ones. This stops you being wicked out by routine wiggles (too-tight stops) or risking too much on a far stop (too-wide). Once the ATR/structure stop distance is set, the <b>position-sizing</b> page turns it into the right number of shares/contracts so the dollar loss equals your fixed risk.`)]
  ],
  edge: [`Trading without a stop exposes you to catastrophic, account-ending losses — it is the #1 way beginners blow up. Always use one.`, `Moving a stop <i>away</i> from price turns a small planned loss into a large one — the most destructive habit in trading.`, `Placing the stop exactly at the obvious level invites a stop-run poke; put it just beyond the zone/wick.`, `Too-tight stops (inside normal noise) get you shaken out of good trades; too-wide stops wreck reward-to-risk — size with ATR/structure.`, `A mental stop you "intend" to honour is not a stop; place the actual order (stop-market for a guaranteed exit).`],
  faq: [['Where exactly should I place my stop loss?', PP(`Just <b>beyond the point that proves your trade idea wrong</b> — for a long, below the swing low or support zone (and below the rejection wick); for a short, above the swing high/resistance. Give the level a little room so a normal wick does not trip it, and avoid placing it <i>exactly</i> at the obvious round level where stop-runs cluster. Cross-check the distance against <b>ATR</b>: if it is far tighter or wider than ~1.5–3×ATR, rethink the entry.`)],
    ['When should I set the stop — and can I move it?', PP(`Set it <b>before you enter</b>, as part of the plan, and place the actual order right away. You may move a stop <b>toward price</b> — to break-even once the trade is working, or to trail and lock in gains — but you must <b>never move it away</b> from price to avoid being stopped out. Widening a stop on a losing trade is the single most dangerous habit; it converts a small, planned loss into a large one.`)],
    ['How do I avoid getting stopped out right before price goes my way?', PP(`Some of that is unavoidable variance, but you reduce it by: placing stops <b>beyond the zone and the wick</b> (not at the obvious level), <b>sizing to ATR</b> so the stop is outside normal noise, and entering <b>close to invalidation</b> (break-and-retest, confirmed candle) so the stop can be tight <i>and</i> meaningful. If you are frequently wicked out, your stops are likely too tight relative to the instrument’s volatility — widen the stop <b>and reduce position size</b> to keep dollar risk the same, rather than removing the stop.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('What is the cardinal rule about an existing stop loss on a losing trade?', ['Move it further away to give the trade room', 'Never move it away from price — only toward price to lock gains', 'Remove it if the loss feels big', 'Double the position instead'], 1, 'Widening a stop turns a small planned loss into a large one — stops only move toward price.'),
    SQ('A stop placed at 2×ATR is designed to…', ['Guarantee a profit', 'Sit beyond normal price noise and adapt to the instrument’s volatility', 'Predict direction', 'Replace position sizing'], 1, 'ATR-based stops give the trade room appropriate to current volatility, avoiding random shakeouts.')]
});
DP('st-stops', {
  fu: [['What is a break-even stop and when do you use it?', 'Moving the stop to your entry price once the trade has moved far enough in your favour (often ~1R of profit), so a winner cannot turn into a loser. Useful, but moving to break-even too early can get you stopped on normal noise — base it on structure (e.g. after a new swing forms), not a fixed tick count.'],
    ['Why do too-tight stops hurt as much as no stop?', 'A stop inside the instrument’s normal noise gets hit by random wiggles, producing frequent small losses and shaking you out of good trades (then watching them work without you). Right-sizing with ATR/structure — and reducing position size rather than tightening the stop — fixes this.'],
    ['What is a guaranteed stop / does slippage matter?', 'A stop-market guarantees execution but not price (gaps/fast moves cause slippage). Some brokers offer guaranteed stops (for a fee) that fill at the exact level. For swing trades held overnight, gap risk means your realised loss can exceed the planned distance — another reason to size conservatively.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Default to a structure stop beyond the swing/level and the wick; validate the distance with ATR; then size the position so that distance equals your fixed risk %. Move the stop only toward price.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'No stop, mental stops not actually placed, widening stops on losers, or stops so tight they get wicked out repeatedly.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Disciplined stop placement and the refusal to widen stops are what keep losses small and survivable; the hit-stop is the system working, not a failure.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: The Stop-Loss Order', 'https://www.investopedia.com/terms/s/stop-lossorder.asp'] });

STT('st-targets', { name: 'Take profit, reward-to-risk & trailing exits', cat: 'ststrat',
  blurb: `Where you take profit decides whether your winners outweigh your losers. This page covers reward-to-risk (the number that makes the math work), placing targets at structure, trailing stops to ride trends, and scaling out — plus moving to break-even.`,
  facts: [['Key number', 'reward-to-risk (R:R)'], ['Minimum', 'aim ~1.5–2R+'], ['Fixed target', 'next support/resistance'], ['Ride trends', 'trailing stop']],
  cheat: [['R (risk)', 'Entry − stop (your 1R); reward measured in R'], ['R:R', 'Reward ÷ risk; target ≥ ~1.5–2× risk'], ['Fixed target', 'Exit at the next S/R / measured move'], ['Trailing stop', 'Ratchet the stop behind price to ride a trend'], ['Scale out', 'Take partial profit, let the rest run'], ['Break-even', 'Move stop to entry after ~1R to remove risk'], ['Structure target', 'Next swing high/low where price stalls'], ['Fib extension', '127/161.8% target zones'], ['Let winners run', 'Trailing lets winners exceed fixed targets'], ['Pre-define', 'Decide the exit before entering']],
  sections: [
    ['Reward-to-risk makes the math work', FIG(chart({ w: 560, h: 250, candles: trend(24, 30, 0.14, 0.9, 6),
      marks: [{ i: 12, p: 31.3, type: 'buy', label: 'entry' }, { i: 0, p: 30.2, type: 'stop', label: 'stop (−1R)' }, { i: 0, p: 33.5, type: 'target', label: 'target (+2R)' }] }),
      'Risk (entry → stop) is 1R; reward (entry → target) is 2R. At 2R, you only need to win ~40% of the time to be profitable. This is why reward-to-risk matters more than win rate.') +
      TBL(['Reward:Risk', 'Breakeven win rate needed'], [['1 : 1', '50%'], ['1.5 : 1', '40%'], ['2 : 1', '~33%'], ['3 : 1', '25%']])],
    ['Three ways to take profit', TBL(['Method', 'How', 'Best for'], [['Fixed target', 'Exit fully at a pre-set level (next S/R, measured move, Fib extension)', 'Simplicity and clean expectancy; ranges'], ['Trailing stop', 'Ratchet the stop behind price (structure or ATR based) and exit when hit', 'Riding trends; capturing big moves'], ['Scale out', 'Take partial profit at the first target, trail the rest', 'Balancing a locked-in win with upside']]) + PP(`A <b>fixed target</b> at the next structure is the simplest and keeps your expectancy measurable. A <b>trailing stop</b> lets winners run in trends but gives back some open profit at the turn. <b>Scaling out</b> is a popular middle ground: bank some at 1–2R, move the stop to break-even, and let a runner chase a bigger move risk-free.`)],
    ['Trailing stops — riding the trend', FIG(chart({ w: 560, h: 230, candles: trend(28, 30, 0.2, 0.8, 4),
      tlines: [{ from: [6, 30.4], to: [26, 34.4], cls: 'stc-sup', dash: true, label: 'trailing stop' }], marks: [{ i: 10, p: 31.5, type: 'buy', label: 'entry' }] }),
      'A trailing stop follows price up (e.g. below each new higher low, or a set ATR distance), locking in more profit as the trend extends and exiting automatically when the trend finally breaks.')],
    ['Setting targets and moving to break-even', UL([`<b>Use structure:</b> the next resistance (long) or support (short) is where price tends to stall — a logical fixed target. Fib extensions (127/161.8%) and pattern measured moves give additional target zones.`, `<b>Check R:R before entry:</b> if the realistic target is less than ~1.5–2× your risk, skip the trade even if the setup is pretty.`, `<b>Break-even:</b> after the trade moves ~1R in your favour (and ideally a new swing forms), consider moving the stop to entry so a winner cannot become a loser — but not so early that noise stops you out.`, `<b>Pre-decide the exit plan</b> (fixed, trail, or scale) and journal it; switching mid-trade based on emotion wrecks expectancy.`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Taking profits too early (cutting winners) while letting losers run is the classic account-killer — it inverts reward-to-risk.`, `A pretty setup with poor R:R (target too close, stop too far) is still a bad trade — measure before entering.`, `Trailing too tight exits on normal noise before the trend really moves; trail on structure or ATR, not ticks.`, `Moving to break-even too early gets you stopped on a wiggle; base it on structure, not impatience.`, `Changing the exit plan mid-trade out of fear/greed destroys the strategy’s expectancy — pre-decide and journal.`],
  faq: [['Why does reward-to-risk matter more than win rate?', PP(`Because it changes the <b>math of survival</b>. At <b>2:1</b> reward-to-risk you only need to win about <b>33%</b> of the time to break even; at 3:1, just 25%. A trader who is "wrong" most of the time can still be very profitable if the wins are multiples of the losses. Conversely, a high win rate with tiny winners and big losers loses money. Always size the target so reward is a healthy multiple of risk — aim for ~1.5–2R or better — and skip setups that cannot offer it.`)],
    ['Should I use a fixed target or a trailing stop?', PP(`Both are valid; it depends on the market and your goal. A <b>fixed target</b> at the next structure is simple, keeps expectancy clean, and suits ranges and quick swings. A <b>trailing stop</b> sacrifices a precise exit to <b>ride trends</b> for larger-than-expected moves, giving back some profit at the turn. Many swing traders <b>scale out</b>: bank partial profit at a fixed target (locking a win), move the stop to break-even, and trail the remainder to chase a runner. Pick one approach per setup and apply it consistently.`)],
    ['When should I move my stop to break-even?', PP(`Once the trade has moved meaningfully in your favour — a common guide is after about <b>1R</b> of profit <i>and</i> once price has formed a new structure (a fresh higher low in an uptrend) to rest the stop under. Moving to break-even removes risk and is psychologically freeing, but doing it <b>too early</b> (on the first wiggle) gets you stopped out of good trades on noise. Tie it to structure, not impatience.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('At a reward-to-risk of 2:1, roughly what win rate do you need just to break even?', ['About 33%', '50%', '70%', '90%'], 0, 'With winners twice the size of losers, ~33% wins breaks even — why R:R beats win rate.'),
    SQ('Which exit approach is best for riding a strong trend to a larger-than-expected move?', ['A tight fixed target', 'A trailing stop (ratcheting behind price)', 'No exit plan', 'Exiting at break-even'], 1, 'A trailing stop lets winners run and exits automatically when the trend finally breaks.')]
});
DP('st-targets', {
  fu: [['What is “cutting winners and letting losers run”?', 'The destructive habit of snatching small profits (fear of giving them back) while holding losers hoping they recover (refusing to accept being wrong). It inverts reward-to-risk — small wins, big losses — and is a leading cause of blown accounts. The cure is pre-defined targets and stops, honoured mechanically.'],
    ['How do you trail a stop objectively?', 'Common methods: below each new higher low (structure trail), a fixed ATR distance below the highest high (chandelier/ATR trail), or below a moving average. Each gives the trend room while ratcheting the stop up; pick one and apply it consistently rather than eyeballing.'],
    ['Does scaling out reduce overall expectancy?', 'It can slightly lower the average win versus letting a full position run to a far target, but it raises consistency and win feel, and locks in profit. It is a trade-off between smoother equity and maximum per-trade gain; journal both to see what suits you.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Pre-define the exit: compute R:R before entry (skip < ~1.5–2R), target the next structure, and choose fixed/trail/scale per setup. Move to break-even on structure after ~1R; trail on structure/ATR in trends.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Cutting winners early, letting losers run, trailing too tight, or changing the exit plan mid-trade on emotion.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Positive expectancy comes from keeping average winners larger than average losers; disciplined, pre-planned exits are how that ratio is maintained across many trades.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Risk/Reward Ratio', 'https://www.investopedia.com/terms/r/riskrewardratio.asp'] });

/* ---------------- Level 6: risk, markets, psychology ---------------- */

STT('st-risk', { name: 'Position sizing & risk management', cat: 'strisk',
  blurb: `This is the most important page in the course. Risk management — how much you risk per trade and how you size positions — is what keeps you in the game long enough for your edge to work. Get this right and mediocre strategies survive; get it wrong and the best strategy still blows up.`,
  facts: [['Risk per trade', 'a small fixed % (≈0.5–1%)'], ['Position size', 'from risk ÷ stop distance'], ['Think in', 'R, not dollars'], ['Goal #1', 'survive drawdowns']],
  cheat: [['1% rule', 'Risk ≤ ~1% of account per trade (0.5–2% range)'], ['Position size', '(account × risk%) ÷ (stop distance per unit)'], ['R', 'The dollars risked per trade; all results in R'], ['Fixed fractional', 'Risk the same % each trade (scales with account)'], ['Max open risk', 'Cap total risk across all open trades'], ['Correlation', 'Correlated trades = one bigger bet; count them together'], ['Drawdown', 'Expect losing streaks; size to survive them'], ['Risk of ruin', 'Over-risking → math guarantees eventual blow-up'], ['Never add to losers', 'Averaging down breaks the plan'], ['Survive first', 'You can’t win if you’re out of the game']],
  sections: [
    ['The 1% rule and why it keeps you alive', PP(`Risk a <b>small, fixed percentage</b> of your account on each trade — commonly around <b>1%</b> (beginners often 0.5%). This caps any single loss and, crucially, survives <b>losing streaks</b>: at 1% risk, even <b>10 losses in a row</b> only draws the account down about 10% — recoverable. At 10% risk, those same 10 losses nearly wipe you out. The entire purpose is <b>staying in the game</b> long enough for your edge’s expectancy to play out over many trades.`) + CO('The math of ruin:', 'Risking too much per trade doesn’t just risk a bad month — it makes eventual ruin a mathematical near-certainty, because a run of losses (which <i>will</i> happen) compounds against a shrinking account.')],
    ['Position sizing: the one formula', PP(`Position size is <b>not</b> "how much I want to buy" — it is calculated so that <b>if the stop is hit, you lose exactly your planned risk</b>:`) +
      TBL(['Step', 'Example'], [['1. Account × risk%', '$10,000 × 1% = $100 risk (1R)'], ['2. Stop distance per unit', 'Entry $50, stop $48 → $2 per share'], ['3. Size = risk ÷ stop distance', '$100 ÷ $2 = 50 shares'], ['4. Check it fits', '50 × $50 = $2,500 position (within limits)']]) +
      PP(`Change any input and the size changes: a <b>wider stop</b> means <b>fewer</b> shares (same dollar risk), a tighter stop means more. This is how a volatile instrument and a calm one can both risk exactly 1% — the stop distance, not the share count, is fixed to the chart; the size adjusts.`)],
    ['Risk across the whole portfolio', UL([`<b>Cap total open risk:</b> if you have several positions, your combined risk should stay within a limit (e.g. 3–6% of the account) — five 1% trades open at once is 5% at risk simultaneously.`, `<b>Account for correlation:</b> three tech stocks (or three USD pairs) moving together are effectively <b>one larger bet</b>; count correlated trades as a single exposure.`, `<b>Expect drawdowns:</b> losing streaks are normal variance, not a broken strategy. Size so the worst plausible streak is survivable and psychologically bearable.`, `<b>Scale risk down</b> when learning, after a losing streak, or in uncertain conditions — never up to "make it back."`])],
    ['Why this page matters most', PP(`A trader with a <b>mediocre</b> strategy and <b>excellent</b> risk management can survive and grind out an edge; a trader with a <b>brilliant</b> strategy and <b>poor</b> risk management will eventually blow up — one oversized trade or one bad streak ends them. You cannot control whether any single trade wins, but you <b>completely control</b> how much you risk. That is why professionals obsess over position sizing and risk, and why beginners who skip it rarely last. <b>Protect the downside and the upside takes care of itself.</b>`)]
  ],
  edge: [`Over-risking per trade makes eventual ruin near-certain — a normal losing streak compounds against a shrinking account.`, `Ignoring correlation stacks hidden risk: five correlated 1% trades can behave like one 5% bet.`, `"Averaging down" (adding to a loser) is the opposite of risk management — it increases risk on a failing idea.`, `Increasing size to "make back" a loss (revenge sizing) is how a bad day becomes a blown account.`, `Risking a fixed dollar amount regardless of stop distance quietly over- or under-risks; size from the stop, every time.`],
  faq: [['How much should I risk per trade?', PP(`A small, <b>fixed percentage</b> — commonly <b>1%</b>, and <b>0.5%</b> or less while learning; rarely above 2% even when experienced. The exact number matters less than that it is <b>small and consistent</b>, so no single trade (and no normal losing streak) can seriously damage the account. At 1% risk, ten consecutive losses cost roughly 10% — a bad patch, not a disaster. This single discipline is the biggest determinant of whether you survive your first year.`)],
    ['How do I calculate position size?', PP(`Work <b>backwards from risk</b>: (1) decide your dollar risk = account × risk% (e.g. $10,000 × 1% = $100). (2) Measure your <b>stop distance</b> per unit (entry minus stop, e.g. $2 per share). (3) <b>Size = risk ÷ stop distance</b> ($100 ÷ $2 = 50 shares). The position size falls out of the chart (where the stop belongs) and your fixed risk — you never pick the size first. A wider stop automatically means a smaller position for the same dollar risk.`)],
    ['Why is risk management more important than the strategy?', PP(`Because you control it completely, and it governs <b>survival</b>. You cannot make any single trade win, but you can guarantee that no single trade (or losing streak) ruins you. A great strategy executed with reckless sizing blows up on its first bad run; a modest strategy with disciplined 1% risk survives to let its small edge compound over hundreds of trades. Staying in the game is the prerequisite for everything else — "protect the downside and the upside takes care of itself."`)]],
  qs: [SQ('You risk 1% per account. Your stop distance is $2 per share and your account is $10,000. How many shares?', ['200 shares', '50 shares', '500 shares', '5 shares'], 1, '$10,000 × 1% = $100 risk; $100 ÷ $2 stop distance = 50 shares.'),
    SQ('Why is risking a small fixed % per trade so important?', ['It maximises each win', 'It ensures no single trade or normal losing streak can ruin the account, so your edge can play out', 'It is required by brokers', 'It guarantees profit'], 1, 'Small, consistent risk keeps you solvent through drawdowns — survival is the point.')]
});
DP('st-risk', {
  fu: [['What is “risk of ruin”?', 'The probability that a string of losses reduces your account to the point you cannot continue. It rises sharply with risk-per-trade and with a negative edge. Small fixed-fractional risk (≈1%) keeps risk of ruin negligible for a strategy with even a slight positive edge; over-risking makes ruin likely regardless of edge.'],
    ['What is fixed-fractional position sizing?', 'Risking the same percentage of the current account on every trade. Position sizes grow as the account grows and shrink after losses, which naturally protects capital in drawdowns and compounds gains in up-streaks — the standard, robust approach for most traders.'],
    ['How does correlation change my real risk?', 'Correlated positions move together, so their risks add rather than diversify. Three long tech stocks each risking 1% can behave like a single 3% position if the sector drops. Count correlated trades as one exposure and cap combined open risk accordingly.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Fix risk% per trade (≈1%), size every position from (account × risk%) ÷ stop distance, cap total and correlated open risk, and reduce size in drawdowns or uncertainty — never increase to recover.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Over-risking, ignoring correlation, averaging down, and revenge-sizing after losses — any one can end an account despite a good strategy.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Position sizing and risk control are the defining habits of professionals; survival through inevitable drawdowns is what lets a small edge compound over hundreds of trades.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Position Sizing & the 1% Rule', 'https://www.investopedia.com/articles/trading/09/risk-management.asp'] });

STT('st-markets-conditions', { name: 'Which markets & conditions suit swing trading', cat: 'strisk',
  blurb: `Swing trading works best in some conditions and fails in others. Knowing when to trade — and when to stand aside — is a skill in itself. This page covers the market types, the volatility you want, and reading the broader environment, directly answering "which market is best for swing trading".`,
  facts: [['Best', 'clear trends & clean ranges'], ['Worst', 'choppy, erratic markets'], ['Want', 'liquidity + moderate volatility'], ['Skill', 'knowing when NOT to trade']],
  cheat: [['Trending market', 'Best for pullback/breakout swings (ideal)'], ['Clean range', 'Good for S/R bounce / mean reversion'], ['Choppy/erratic', 'Worst — stand aside or trade tiny'], ['Liquid', 'Tight spreads, reliable fills — required'], ['Moderate volatility', 'Enough to move, not chaos'], ['Low volatility (squeeze)', 'Prepare for a breakout'], ['Extreme volatility/news', 'Wider stops or stay out'], ['Market regime', 'Broad index up/down sets the tone'], ['Sector strength', 'Trade strong stocks in strong sectors'], ['Stand aside', 'No setup, no trade — a valid position']],
  sections: [
    ['The three market conditions (and what to do)', FIG2(
      FIG(chart({ w: 270, h: 180, candles: trend(20, 20, 0.24, 0.6, 3), notes: [{ i: 10, p: 19.0, text: 'TREND — ideal', anchor: 'middle' }] }), 'Trend: clean HH/HL. Trade pullbacks and with-trend breakouts — the best swing conditions.'),
      FIG(chart({ w: 270, h: 180, candles: trend(24, 22, 0, 1.3, 11), hlines: [{ p: 24.1, cls: 'stc-res' }, { p: 19.9, cls: 'stc-sup' }], notes: [{ i: 12, p: 18.9, text: 'RANGE — tradable', anchor: 'middle' }] }), 'Range: oscillates between levels. Trade the edges (buy support, sell resistance).')) +
      FIG(chart({ w: 560, h: 150, candles: trend(30, 20, 0, 2.6, 13), notes: [{ i: 15, p: 15.8, text: 'CHOP — stand aside', anchor: 'middle' }] }), 'Chop: large, overlapping, directionless candles. The hardest condition — whipsaws kill both trend and range tactics. Often best to not trade.')],
    ['What makes a market good to swing trade', TBL(['Property', 'You want…', 'Avoid'], [['Liquidity', 'High (tight spreads, clean fills)', 'Thin, illiquid instruments'], ['Volatility', 'Moderate — real moves to capture', 'Dead-flat (no moves) or chaotic (random stops)'], ['Structure', 'Respects levels and trends', 'Erratic, news-whipsawed chop'], ['Direction', 'A clear trend or a clean range', 'Directionless churn'], ['Catalyst risk', 'Known and manageable', 'Imminent binary events (earnings) while in a trade']]) + PP(`Swing trading needs <b>movement with order</b>: enough volatility to produce swings, enough structure that levels and trends mean something. The worst environment is a choppy, erratic market — plenty of movement, no reliability.`)],
    ['Reading the broader environment', UL([`<b>Market regime:</b> in stocks, the broad index (e.g. the overall market) sets the tide — most stocks fall in a falling market. Favour longs when the index is in an uptrend, be cautious or favour shorts when it is weak.`, `<b>Sector/relative strength:</b> trade the <b>strongest</b> names in the <b>strongest</b> sectors for longs (and weakest for shorts) — relative strength tends to persist.`, `<b>Volatility regime:</b> a market-wide volatility spike (fear) means wider swings and gaps — widen stops, cut size, or stand aside. A squeeze (calm) hints a bigger move is coming.`, `<b>Event risk:</b> avoid holding through binary events (earnings, major announcements) unless that is your deliberate strategy — gaps can jump your stop.`])],
    ['The skill of standing aside', PP(`There is no rule that you must have a position. When the market is <b>choppy</b>, when there is <b>no clean setup</b>, or when a <b>major event</b> looms, the highest-probability "trade" is often <b>no trade</b>. Overtrading in poor conditions is how edges get given back. Patience — waiting for the market to come into a condition your strategy is built for — is itself a core skill. Cash is a position, and preserving capital in bad conditions is how you have capital for the good ones.`)]
  ],
  edge: [`Choppy, erratic markets whipsaw both trend and range strategies — forcing trades there bleeds the account.`, `Trading illiquid instruments brings wide spreads, bad fills and gap risk; stick to liquid markets.`, `Fighting the broader market regime (buying weak stocks in a falling market) lowers your odds.`, `Holding through earnings/major events invites gaps that jump your stop — know the calendar.`, `The urge to "always be in a trade" (overtrading) in poor conditions is a major, underrated account-killer.`],
  faq: [['What is the best market condition for swing trading?', PP(`A <b>clear, orderly trend</b> is ideal — it gives repeatable pullback and breakout entries with the odds on your side. A <b>clean range</b> is the next best, traded at its edges (buy support, sell resistance). The condition to <b>avoid</b> is <b>choppy, erratic</b> price — lots of movement but no structure, where both trend and range tactics get whipsawed. In short: you want <b>movement with order</b>, and liquid instruments with moderate volatility.`)],
    ['How much volatility do I actually want?', PP(`<b>Moderate</b> — enough that price makes real, tradable swings, but not so much that normal noise blows through sensible stops and gaps are constant. Too little volatility means no profit potential; too much (a news-driven, erratic tape) means random stop-outs and unpredictable gaps. Use <b>ATR</b> to judge an instrument’s typical movement and size stops to it; when volatility spikes market-wide, widen stops and cut size, or stand aside.`)],
    ['Should I always be in a trade?', PP(`No — and believing you should is a common, costly mistake. The market is only in a condition suited to your strategy <i>some</i> of the time; the rest of the time the right move is to <b>wait</b>. Standing aside in choppy conditions, when no clean setup exists, or ahead of major events protects capital for the high-probability setups. <b>Cash is a position</b>, patience is an edge, and overtrading poor conditions is how good traders give back profits.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('Which condition is the hardest / usually best avoided for swing trading?', ['A clear trend', 'A choppy, erratic, directionless market', 'A clean range', 'Moderate volatility'], 1, 'Choppy markets whipsaw both trend and range tactics — often best to stand aside.'),
    SQ('In a falling broad market, a swing trader should generally…', ['Aggressively buy the weakest stocks', 'Be cautious with longs and respect the regime (favour strength, or shorts)', 'Ignore the broader market', 'Remove all stops'], 1, 'Most stocks follow the market tide; trading against the regime lowers your odds.')]
});
DP('st-markets-conditions', {
  fu: [['What is relative strength (not the RSI)?', 'How an instrument performs versus a benchmark (e.g. a stock vs the index). Stocks showing strong relative strength tend to lead rallies and fall less in dips; trading the strongest names in strong sectors (and weakest for shorts) stacks the odds via persistence of relative strength.'],
    ['How do you tell a range from the start of a trend?', 'A range shows repeated rejections at the same highs and lows with overlapping candles; a nascent trend starts making higher highs/lows (or lower) and breaks a range boundary with momentum/volume. Until a breakout confirms, treat sideways action as a range.'],
    ['Why avoid holding through earnings?', 'Earnings (and similar binary events) can gap price far beyond your stop overnight, turning a controlled risk into a large, un-stoppable loss (or gain). Unless event-trading is your deliberate, sized-for-it strategy, flat or reduced exposure into known events is prudent risk management.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Classify the condition (trend/range/chop) and the regime (index/sector/volatility) first, then apply the matching strategy — or stand aside. Favour liquid instruments with moderate, ATR-measured volatility.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Overtrading chop, fighting the market regime, trading illiquid names, and holding through binary events.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Selectivity — trading only when conditions suit the strategy, in strong instruments, and sitting out the rest — is a defining habit of consistent swing traders.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Market Conditions & Trading', 'https://www.investopedia.com/articles/trading/09/swing-trading.asp'] });

STT('st-process', { name: 'Finding trades: multi-timeframe, scanning & watchlists', cat: 'strisk',
  blurb: `A repeatable process turns thousands of instruments into a short list of A-plus setups. This page covers the top-down multi-timeframe routine, how to scan for candidates, and how to run a watchlist — the daily workflow that feeds every strategy.`,
  facts: [['Top-down', 'weekly → daily → entry TF'], ['Scan', 'filter many → a few candidates'], ['Watchlist', 'pre-planned trades with levels'], ['Routine', 'a few minutes, daily']],
  cheat: [['Weekly', 'Trend & major levels (context)'], ['Daily', 'The setup & your decision'], ['4h/1h', 'Entry timing (optional)'], ['Scan filters', 'Liquidity, trend, near a level, pattern'], ['Watchlist', 'Instruments at or near a setup'], ['Alerts', 'Price alerts at your levels (hands-off)'], ['Pre-plan', 'Entry, stop, target noted before the day'], ['Top-down', 'Higher TF direction first, always'], ['Few quality', 'A short list beats a long one'], ['Daily routine', 'Scan → update watchlist → set alerts/orders']],
  sections: [
    ['The top-down multi-timeframe routine', OL([`<b>Weekly (context):</b> what is the big trend, and where are the major support/resistance levels? Mark them.`, `<b>Daily (setup):</b> is there a tradable setup <i>in line with</i> the weekly trend — a pullback to support, a breakout, a reversal at a major level?`, `<b>Lower TF (entry, optional):</b> drop to the 4-hour or 1-hour only to <b>time</b> the entry (a reversal candle, a retest) within the daily setup.`, `<b>Decide and pre-plan:</b> note the entry, stop and target, check the reward-to-risk, and place orders or alerts. Then let it work.`]) + PP(`The golden rule again: the <b>higher timeframe sets direction</b>, the lower one only times the entry. A daily setup fighting the weekly trend is a weaker trade.`)],
    ['Scanning: from thousands to a handful', PP(`You cannot watch every instrument, so you <b>filter</b>. A scan narrows the universe to candidates worth looking at — for example: liquid enough (minimum volume), in an uptrend (price above a rising 50-MA), and <b>near a level or forming a pattern</b> (close to support, at a 52-week high, inside a squeeze). Free and paid scanners let you set these filters. The scan does not pick trades — it produces a <b>short list</b> you then analyse by hand with your top-down routine.`)],
    ['Running a watchlist', UL([`Keep a <b>small watchlist</b> of instruments at or approaching a setup, each annotated with the <b>level, trigger, stop and target</b> you are waiting for.`, `Set <b>price alerts</b> at your levels so you do not have to stare at screens — swing trading is hands-off by design.`, `Each day/evening: <b>scan</b> for new candidates, <b>update</b> the watchlist (remove invalidated ones, add new), and <b>set alerts/orders</b>.`, `Quality over quantity: a focused watchlist of well-understood setups beats a sprawling list you cannot track.`])],
    ['The daily workflow', PP(`Swing trading’s routine is short and repeatable: <b>scan</b> for setups → <b>analyse</b> candidates top-down → <b>pre-plan</b> the trades (levels + R:R) → <b>set alerts and orders</b> → <b>manage</b> open positions (trail, move to break-even, honour stops) → <b>journal</b>. Most of this takes minutes a day because the orders are pre-placed and the daily chart does not require constant attention. The discipline is in doing it <b>consistently</b> and only acting on setups that pass your filters.`)]
  ],
  edge: [`Skipping the higher-timeframe check leads to trading against the dominant trend — always go top-down.`, `A scan output is a candidate list, not a trade list — still analyse each by hand; blindly trading scan hits is low quality.`, `An oversized watchlist you cannot track leads to missed management and rushed decisions — keep it small.`, `Watching screens all day for a daily-chart strategy invites overtrading and emotional decisions; use alerts.`, `No pre-planned levels means improvising in the moment — decide entry/stop/target before the trade.`],
  faq: [['What is the top-down (multi-timeframe) approach?', PP(`Analyse from the <b>highest timeframe down</b>: the <b>weekly</b> for the big trend and major levels (context), the <b>daily</b> for the actual setup and decision, and optionally a <b>lower timeframe</b> (4h/1h) only to time the entry. This keeps you trading <i>with</i> the dominant force and finding precise, lower-risk entries. The higher timeframe always wins ties: a daily setup against the weekly trend is lower-probability.`)],
    ['How do I find setups among thousands of instruments?', PP(`Use a <b>scanner</b> to filter the universe down to candidates that meet your criteria — e.g. sufficient liquidity, in a trend (above a rising MA), and near a level or forming a pattern (near support, at new highs, in a squeeze). The scan produces a <b>short list</b>; you then analyse each by hand with your top-down routine and keep the best on a watchlist. Scanning turns an impossible amount of data into a manageable few quality setups.`)],
    ['How much time does swing trading actually take daily?', PP(`Typically <b>a few minutes to perhaps half an hour</b> a day, because the work is front-loaded and the orders are pre-placed. A common rhythm: once a day (often after the close) you scan, update your watchlist, set alerts and orders, and manage open trades. You do <b>not</b> need to watch screens intraday — in fact, doing so tends to cause overtrading. Alerts bring the opportunities to you.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('In the top-down routine, which timeframe determines the direction you trade?', ['The lowest timeframe', 'The higher timeframe (weekly for context, then daily)', 'Whichever confirms your bias', 'The 1-minute'], 1, 'Higher timeframe sets direction; lower timeframes only time the entry.'),
    SQ('What is the purpose of a scanner?', ['To automatically place trades', 'To filter many instruments down to a short list of candidates you then analyse', 'To predict prices', 'To replace stop losses'], 1, 'Scans narrow the universe to candidates; you still analyse each by hand before trading.')]
});
DP('st-process', {
  fu: [['Should lower timeframes ever override the higher timeframe?', 'No — the lower timeframe is for timing, not direction. If the daily/weekly trend is up, you use the lower timeframe to find a good long entry, not to justify a short. When they conflict, defer to the higher timeframe or stand aside.'],
    ['What filters make a good stock scan?', 'Common ones: minimum average volume (liquidity), price above/below a key MA (trend), proximity to a 52-week high/low or a defined level, a volatility squeeze, or a specific pattern. Combine a liquidity filter with a trend and a location filter to surface tradable candidates.'],
    ['Why use alerts instead of watching live?', 'Alerts let the setup come to you, which suits the daily-chart, days-to-weeks horizon and prevents the overtrading and emotional decisions that constant screen-watching causes. You act only when price reaches a pre-planned level.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Daily loop: scan → top-down analyse candidates → pre-plan entry/stop/target and R:R → set alerts/orders → manage open trades → journal. Keep the watchlist small and the higher-timeframe trend primary.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Trading scan hits blindly, ignoring the higher timeframe, oversized watchlists, and screen-watching that breeds overtrading.'],
    ['In real trading', 'A consistent top-down process with scanning and alerts is what lets swing traders cover many markets in minutes a day and act only on quality, pre-planned setups.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Multiple Time Frame Analysis', 'https://www.investopedia.com/articles/trading/07/timeframes.asp'] });

STT('st-backtest', { name: 'Backtesting & building a system', cat: 'strisk',
  blurb: `Before risking money, prove your setup has an edge — and keep proving it. Backtesting (on past data) and forward-testing (on new data, on paper) turn "this looks good" into measured expectancy, and guard against fooling yourself with hindsight.`,
  facts: [['Backtest', 'test rules on past data'], ['Forward test', 'test on new/paper data'], ['Metric', 'expectancy (avg R per trade)'], ['Danger', 'curve-fitting to the past']],
  cheat: [['Backtest', 'Apply exact rules to historical data'], ['Sample size', '50–100+ trades for meaningful stats'], ['Expectancy', 'Average R per trade (the key metric)'], ['Win rate', 'Only meaningful alongside R:R'], ['Max drawdown', 'Worst peak-to-trough — can you survive it?'], ['Profit factor', 'Gross profit ÷ gross loss (>1 = profitable)'], ['Forward test', 'Paper trade new data (out of sample)'], ['Curve fitting', 'Over-tuning to the past — fails live'], ['Keep rules simple', 'Robust beats optimised'], ['One change at a time', 'Isolate what actually helps']],
  sections: [
    ['Backtest, then forward test', OL([`<b>Define exact rules:</b> entry, stop, target, and filters — no ambiguity, so the test is repeatable.`, `<b>Backtest on past data:</b> step through history (manually candle-by-candle, or with software) applying the rules to a large sample (<b>50–100+ trades</b>), recording each result in R.`, `<b>Compute the metrics:</b> expectancy (avg R/trade), win rate, reward-to-risk, max drawdown, profit factor.`, `<b>Forward test (out of sample):</b> paper trade the rules on <b>new</b> data you did not use to build them — the real test that the edge is not just hindsight.`, `<b>Go live small</b> only if forward results hold, then scale slowly (ties back to the trading plan).`])],
    ['The metrics that matter', TBL(['Metric', 'What it tells you', 'Watch for'], [['Expectancy (avg R)', 'Average profit per trade in R — the core number', 'Must be clearly positive over a large sample'], ['Win rate', '% of trades that profit', 'Meaningless without R:R; high win rate can still lose'], ['Reward-to-risk', 'Average winner vs average loser', 'The other half of expectancy'], ['Max drawdown', 'Worst peak-to-trough decline', 'Can you survive it financially AND emotionally?'], ['Profit factor', 'Gross profit ÷ gross loss', '>1 profitable; the higher the better']]) + PP(`<b>Expectancy</b> is the headline: a positive average R per trade over a large, out-of-sample set is what a real edge looks like. A pretty equity curve on in-sample data proves nothing.`)],
    ['The great danger: curve fitting', PP(`The biggest trap in testing is <b>over-optimisation (curve fitting)</b>: tweaking rules and settings until they look perfect on <i>past</i> data. The result fits historical <b>noise</b>, not a durable edge, and collapses on live data. Guard against it by: keeping rules <b>simple</b> (few parameters), testing across <b>many instruments and time periods</b>, changing <b>one variable at a time</b>, and always validating on <b>out-of-sample</b> data. A robust, slightly-less-optimal system beats a perfectly-fitted fragile one every time.`)],
    ['Honest testing', UL([`<b>No hindsight:</b> only use information you would have had in real time (don’t peek ahead).`, `<b>Include costs:</b> spreads, commissions and realistic slippage — they turn some "profitable" systems negative.`, `<b>Account for gaps:</b> model that stops can be jumped on gaps (losses sometimes exceed the planned R).`, `<b>Big enough sample:</b> a handful of trades is noise; you need 50–100+ to trust the numbers, more for low-frequency setups.`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Curve fitting (over-optimising to past data) is the #1 testing mistake — it looks perfect in backtest and fails live.`, `Small samples are noise; 5–10 trades tell you almost nothing about expectancy.`, `Ignoring costs and slippage makes marginal systems look profitable when they are not.`, `Hindsight bias (using info you wouldn’t have had live) inflates backtest results — be strict.`, `A good backtest is necessary but not sufficient; forward (out-of-sample) testing is the real proof.`],
  faq: [['Why isn’t a good backtest enough?', PP(`Because you built the rules <i>knowing</i> the past, so it is easy — often unconsciously — to fit them to that specific history (curve fitting). A backtest can look flawless yet fail live. The real test is <b>out-of-sample</b>: forward-test the fixed rules on <b>new</b> data (paper trading) that played no part in designing them. If the edge survives data it has never seen, it is more likely real. Treat the backtest as a first filter, not proof.`)],
    ['What is the single most important backtest metric?', PP(`<b>Expectancy</b> — the average R (risk multiples) the system returns per trade over a large sample. A clearly positive expectancy across many out-of-sample trades is what a genuine edge looks like. Win rate alone is misleading (a high win rate with big losers loses money), and a pretty equity curve on in-sample data proves nothing. Also check <b>max drawdown</b> — can you survive the worst stretch financially and emotionally?`)],
    ['How many trades do I need to trust the results?', PP(`Aim for <b>50–100+</b> trades as a rough minimum, and more for low-frequency setups — enough that normal variance averages out and the expectancy is meaningful. Any 5–10 trades are dominated by luck. Combine a large sample with out-of-sample validation and realistic costs, and be wary of drawing strong conclusions from a short, cherry-picked period.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('What is the biggest danger when backtesting a strategy?', ['Using too much data', 'Curve fitting — over-optimising to past data so it fails live', 'Testing on stocks', 'Measuring expectancy'], 1, 'Over-fitting to historical noise produces a perfect backtest that collapses on new data.'),
    SQ('Why is out-of-sample (forward) testing essential?', ['It is faster', 'It validates the fixed rules on new data that didn’t shape them — the real proof of an edge', 'It guarantees profit', 'It replaces risk management'], 1, 'An edge that survives unseen data is far more likely to be real than one fitted to the past.')]
});
DP('st-backtest', {
  fu: [['What is walk-forward testing?', 'Optimising on one slice of data, then testing on the next unseen slice, rolling forward repeatedly. It approximates how a strategy would have adapted over time and is more robust than a single in-sample optimisation, reducing curve-fitting risk.'],
    ['Why include slippage and costs?', 'Spreads, commissions and slippage are real per-trade drags that compound over many trades. A system with a thin edge can be profitable before costs and negative after — omitting them is a common way backtests mislead.'],
    ['Manual vs automated backtesting?', 'Manual (stepping through charts) builds pattern-recognition skill and intuition and suits discretionary setups; automated is faster and more objective for rule-based systems but risks over-optimisation. Many traders start manual to learn, then automate robust rules.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Define exact rules, backtest on a large sample with realistic costs and gap handling, measure expectancy/drawdown/profit factor, then forward-test out-of-sample before going live small.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Curve fitting, tiny samples, hindsight bias, and omitting costs — each inflates backtest results and breeds false confidence.'],
    ['In real practice', 'Robust, simple systems validated out-of-sample and traded with strict risk control outperform heavily-optimised fragile ones; expectancy over a large sample is the truth-teller.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Backtesting', 'https://www.investopedia.com/terms/b/backtesting.asp'] });

STT('st-psychology', { name: 'Trading psychology & discipline', cat: 'strisk',
  blurb: `You can know every pattern and still lose, because trading is executed by a human under fear and greed. Discipline — following your plan when emotions scream otherwise — is what separates consistent traders from the rest. This page covers the mental game and how to manage it.`,
  facts: [['Enemies', 'fear, greed, FOMO, tilt'], ['Edge', 'discipline & patience'], ['Tool', 'the plan & journal'], ['Protect', 'from your own impulses']],
  cheat: [['Fear', 'Cutting winners early, hesitating on valid setups'], ['Greed', 'Over-sizing, chasing, ignoring targets'], ['FOMO', 'Chasing moves you missed — worst entries'], ['Tilt', 'Emotional revenge-trading after a loss'], ['Hope', 'Holding losers past the stop'], ['Overconfidence', 'Over-sizing after a winning streak'], ['Discipline', 'Following the plan regardless of feelings'], ['Patience', 'Waiting for A-plus setups; cash is a position'], ['Process > outcome', 'Judge decisions, not single results'], ['Rules for emotion', 'Walk away when tired/angry/on tilt']],
  sections: [
    ['The emotions that cost money', TBL(['Emotion', 'How it shows up', 'The damage'], [['Fear', 'Cutting winners early; skipping valid setups; too-tight stops', 'Small winners, missed trades'], ['Greed', 'Over-sizing; chasing; holding past the target', 'Big losses; giving back profit'], ['FOMO', 'Chasing a move after it ran', 'Terrible entries, wide stops'], ['Hope', 'Moving/removing stops; holding losers', 'Small losses become large'], ['Revenge / tilt', 'Trading angrily to "make it back"', 'Rapid, oversized, rule-breaking losses'], ['Overconfidence', 'Over-sizing after wins', 'One big loss erases many wins']]) + PP(`Notice that most of these are the <b>same rule-breaks</b> the risk and exit pages warn against — fear and greed are <i>why</i> traders break good rules. Managing the emotions is how you keep the rules.`)],
    ['Process over outcome', PP(`A single trade’s result is mostly <b>noise</b> — a good decision can lose and a bad decision can win. If you judge yourself by individual outcomes, you will "learn" the wrong lessons (e.g. that breaking your rules "worked"). Instead, judge the <b>quality of your decisions</b>: did I take a valid setup, size it correctly, and manage it per plan? Over many trades, good process produces good outcomes. This reframing — <b>process over outcome</b> — is the foundation of trading discipline and is exactly what the journal measures.`)],
    ['Building discipline', UL([`<b>Have a written plan and follow it</b> — the plan is your pre-committed, rational self protecting you from your in-the-moment emotional self.`, `<b>Small risk per trade</b> lowers the emotional stakes, which makes it far easier to follow rules; most tilt comes from positions that are too big.`, `<b>Rules for your state:</b> do not trade when tired, angry, or on tilt; step away after a big loss or a losing streak.`, `<b>Journal the emotions</b>, not just the trades — spotting your recurring triggers (revenge after losses, FOMO at new highs) is how you fix them.`, `<b>Accept losses as a cost of business:</b> a stopped-out trade executed per plan is a <i>success</i> of discipline, not a failure.`])],
    ['Patience as an edge', PP(`Much of trading is <b>waiting</b> — for the setup, for the trigger, for the trade to work. The urge to <b>act</b> (to always be in a position, to chase, to tinker) is the enemy. The best traders are comfortable doing <b>nothing</b> until an A-plus setup appears, and comfortable letting a good trade work without meddling. Overtrading — born of boredom, FOMO, or revenge — gives back more profit than almost anything else. <b>Patience and discipline are not soft skills; they are the edge</b> that lets your technical knowledge actually make money.`)]
  ],
  edge: [`Revenge trading after a loss (tilt) causes rapid, oversized, rule-breaking losses — have a rule to stop and walk away.`, `Over-sizing after a winning streak (overconfidence) sets up one big loss that erases many wins.`, `Judging yourself by single-trade outcomes teaches wrong lessons — a rule-break that "won" is still a bad decision.`, `FOMO produces the worst entries (chasing); if you missed it, wait for the next setup.`, `Positions that are too large are the root of most emotional mistakes — small risk restores discipline.`],
  faq: [['Why do traders who know the techniques still lose?', PP(`Because trading is <b>executed under emotion</b>. Fear makes you cut winners and skip valid setups; greed makes you over-size and chase; hope makes you hold losers past the stop; tilt makes you revenge-trade. These feelings cause the <b>rule-breaks</b> that the risk and exit lessons warn against. Knowledge is necessary but not sufficient — <b>discipline</b> (following the plan when emotions scream otherwise) is the deciding skill, and it is a muscle you build, largely by keeping risk small and journaling.`)],
    ['What does "process over outcome" mean?', PP(`Judge your trading by the <b>quality of your decisions</b>, not the result of any single trade. Because each trade is partly luck, a good, by-the-plan decision can lose and a reckless one can win. If you reward yourself for outcomes, you will reinforce bad habits that happened to work. By focusing on process — valid setup, correct size, managed per plan — you make the choices that produce good <i>aggregate</i> outcomes over hundreds of trades, and you stay sane through normal losing streaks.`)],
    ['How do I stop revenge trading and overtrading?', PP(`Three things help most: (1) <b>keep risk per trade small</b> — most tilt comes from positions big enough to hurt; small risk lowers the emotional stakes. (2) <b>Make explicit rules for your mental state</b> — e.g. stop trading for the day after two losses or any rule-break, and never trade tired or angry. (3) <b>Journal your emotions</b> to see your triggers (chasing at new highs, doubling down after losses) and treat patience — doing nothing until an A-plus setup — as a skill you are practising. Cash is a position.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('What does "process over outcome" mean for a trader?', ['Only the outcome matters', 'Judge the quality of your decisions, not any single trade’s result, since each trade is partly luck', 'Ignore your plan', 'Trade more often'], 1, 'Good process produces good aggregate results; single outcomes are noisy and teach false lessons if over-weighted.'),
    SQ('What most effectively reduces emotional, rule-breaking trades?', ['Larger positions for bigger wins', 'Keeping risk per trade small, plus rules for your mental state', 'Removing stop losses', 'Trading on every setup'], 1, 'Small risk lowers emotional stakes; combined with state-based rules it keeps discipline intact.')]
});
DP('st-psychology', {
  fu: [['What is “tilt” and how do you break it?', 'Tilt (from poker) is emotionally compromised decision-making, usually after a loss — trading angrily to "get it back." Break it with a hard rule: stop trading after a defined trigger (e.g. two losses, any rule-break, or a daily loss limit), step away, and resume only when calm. Pre-commit to the rule when rational.'],
    ['Why does small position size improve psychology?', 'Emotions scale with money at stake. A position small enough that a loss is trivial is easy to manage by the rules; a position large enough to hurt triggers fear and greed that cause rule-breaks. Right-sizing is as much a psychological tool as a mathematical one.'],
    ['How does the journal help the mental game?', 'By recording emotions and rule-adherence alongside results, it reveals your personal failure patterns (chasing, revenge, cutting winners) and proves, over time, that disciplined process pays and impulsive deviations cost — turning abstract advice into personal evidence you believe.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Pre-commit to rules when calm (plan, risk %, state-based stop-trading triggers), keep risk small, journal emotions, and judge yourself on process. Treat patience and sitting out as active skills.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Tilt/revenge trading, overconfidence after wins, FOMO chasing, and judging by single outcomes — all amplified by oversized positions.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Discipline and emotional control are consistently cited by professionals as more decisive than strategy; the edge is useless if emotion prevents executing it.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Trading Psychology', 'https://www.investopedia.com/articles/trading/02/110502.asp'] });

STT('st-together', { name: 'Putting it all together: a full swing trade', cat: 'strisk',
  blurb: `This page walks one complete trade end to end — from scanning to journaling — showing how trend, levels, candles, indicators, entry, stop, target, sizing and psychology combine into a single disciplined decision. It is the whole course in one example.`,
  facts: [['Flow', 'scan → analyse → plan → execute → manage → journal'], ['Decision', 'confluence of several factors'], ['Risk', 'fixed % sized from the stop'], ['Judge', 'the process, not the result']],
  cheat: [['1 Scan', 'Filter for liquid, trending candidates near a level'], ['2 Top-down', 'Weekly trend & levels → daily setup'], ['3 Confluence', 'Support + MA + Fib + reversal candle + volume'], ['4 Plan', 'Entry, stop, target; check R:R ≥ ~2'], ['5 Size', '(account × 1%) ÷ stop distance'], ['6 Orders', 'Limit/stop entry, stop-market stop, target'], ['7 Manage', 'Break-even, trail or scale out per plan'], ['8 Exit', 'Target hit, stop hit, or trail stop'], ['9 Journal', 'Screenshot, R result, emotions, rule-adherence'], ['Repeat', 'Consistently, over many trades']],
  sections: [
    ['The complete setup, drawn', FIG(chart({ w: 560, h: 270, candles: trend(18, 48, 0.18, 0.7, 4).concat(trend(6, 51.0, -0.3, 0.4, 9), [[49.6, 50.1, 48.9, 50.0], [50.0, 50.9, 49.9, 50.8], [50.8, 51.6, 50.6, 51.5], [51.5, 52.3, 51.3, 52.2]]),
      lines: [{ data: (() => { const c = trend(18, 48, 0.18, 0.7, 4).concat(trend(6, 51.0, -0.3, 0.4, 9), [[49.6, 50.1, 48.9, 50.0], [50.0, 50.9, 49.9, 50.8], [50.8, 51.6, 50.6, 51.5], [51.5, 52.3, 51.3, 52.2]]); return ema(closesOf(c), 20); })(), cls: 'stc-c1 stc-thin', label: '20 EMA' }],
      hlines: [{ p: 49.6, label: 'support', cls: 'stc-sup' }],
      marks: [{ i: 24, p: 50.15, type: 'buy', label: 'entry: reversal candle at support+EMA' }, { i: 0, p: 48.7, type: 'stop', label: 'stop: below support (−1R)' }, { i: 0, p: 53.0, type: 'target', label: 'target: +2.3R next resistance' }] }),
      'Uptrend → pullback into a confluence zone (support + the rising 20-EMA) → a bullish reversal candle on rising volume triggers the entry. Stop below support (1R); target the next resistance (~2.3R). Everything you learned, in one chart.')],
    ['Walking the trade, step by step', OL([`<b>Scan & top-down:</b> a liquid stock in a clear uptrend (weekly up, price above a rising 50-MA) pulls back on the daily toward a support level that <b>coincides</b> with the rising 20-EMA and a 50% Fibonacci retracement — <b>confluence</b>.`, `<b>Trigger:</b> at that zone, a <b>bullish engulfing</b> candle closes on a volume uptick (momentum turning, participation confirming).`, `<b>Plan & R:R:</b> entry on the break of the candle’s high ($50.15), stop just below support ($48.70 = <b>1R</b> of $1.45), target the next resistance ($53.00 ≈ <b>2.3R</b>). R:R passes the ~2 minimum.`, `<b>Size:</b> on a $10,000 account risking 1% ($100), size = $100 ÷ $1.45 ≈ <b>69 shares</b> — so a stop-out loses ~$100, no matter the share price.`, `<b>Execute & manage:</b> place the orders; after ~1R move the stop to break-even once a new higher low forms; then trail under structure (or scale out) to ride the up-swing.`, `<b>Exit & journal:</b> target hit, trail hit, or stop hit — whatever happens, record the screenshot, the result in R, your emotions, and whether you followed the plan.`])],
    ['Why the result of this one trade barely matters', PP(`Suppose this trade <b>loses</b> — the engulfing fails and the stop is hit for −1R (~$100, 1% of the account). That is <b>not</b> a failure: it was a valid, high-confluence setup, correctly sized, managed by the plan. The <b>process was sound</b>; the outcome was variance. Run this same disciplined process across <b>100 trades</b> with a positive expectancy and small fixed risk, and the account grows despite many individual losses. <b>That</b> is swing trading: not predicting this trade, but executing a positive-edge process, sized to survive, over and over.`)],
    ['Your path from here', OL([`<b>Learn the pieces</b> (the earlier units) until chart-reading, patterns, indicators, stops, targets and sizing are second nature.`, `<b>Write your plan</b> with one or two setups fully defined.`, `<b>Backtest and paper trade</b> it to a positive, out-of-sample expectancy.`, `<b>Go live tiny</b>, journaling every trade and judging your <b>process</b>.`, `<b>Scale slowly</b> as consistency proves out — and keep risk small, forever.`]) + RISK],
  ],
  edge: [`Skipping any step (no R:R check, no sizing, no journal) breaks the chain — the edge lives in the <i>whole</i> process, executed consistently.`, `Judging this single trade by its win/loss instead of by process quality teaches the wrong lesson.`, `Jumping to live trading with real size before proving expectancy and building discipline is the most common path to blowing up.`, `Chasing a "perfect" setup with poor risk control still loses; chasing many mediocre ones loses faster.`, `Inconsistency — changing the process trade to trade — prevents any edge from ever showing.`],
  faq: [['What actually makes this a high-probability trade?', PP(`<b>Confluence plus discipline.</b> Several independent factors align at one place — the higher-timeframe uptrend, a support level, the rising 20-EMA, a Fibonacci retracement, a confirming reversal candle, and supporting volume — so the odds are better than any single signal. Then <b>risk management</b> makes it <i>worth</i> taking: a tight, logical stop and a ~2.3R target give a favourable payoff, and 1% sizing ensures a loss is trivial. High-confluence entry + strong reward-to-risk + small fixed risk is the recipe.`)],
    ['If I do everything right and still lose, what did I do wrong?', PP(`<b>Nothing.</b> A sound, by-the-plan trade can lose — markets are probabilistic, and even great setups fail a meaningful share of the time. The loss was pre-defined and small (1R ≈ 1% of the account). The correct response is to <b>journal it as a good decision with a losing outcome</b> and take the next valid setup. Judging yourself by single outcomes (rather than process) is itself the mistake. Edge shows over <b>many</b> trades, not one.`)],
    ['How long until I can trade this for real?', PP(`There is no fixed timeline, and anyone promising a quick one is a red flag. Realistically, plan on <b>months</b>: learn the concepts, write and backtest a plan, <b>paper trade</b> it to a positive out-of-sample expectancy, then trade <b>tiny</b> real size while journaling, and scale only as consistency proves out. Many traders are net negative in their first year — that is normal. Rushing to large size before the process and discipline are proven is the single biggest cause of failure. Go slow; survival first.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('In the worked trade, why does its individual win/loss "barely matter"?', ['Because the trade was risk-free', 'Because a positive-expectancy process executed consistently over many trades is what produces results; one outcome is variance', 'Because stops do not work', 'Because the account was large'], 1, 'Edge shows over many trades; a single sound, small-risk trade’s result is noise, not a verdict on the process.'),
    SQ('What is the correct sequence for a full swing trade?', ['Buy → hope → sell', 'Scan → top-down analyse → plan (R:R) → size → execute → manage → journal', 'Pick a stock → go all in', 'Trade first, plan later'], 1, 'The disciplined end-to-end process is what contains risk and lets the edge play out.')]
});
DP('st-together', {
  fu: [['Where do the earlier units show up in this one trade?', 'Trend & structure (uptrend), support/resistance and MAs (the confluence zone), candlesticks (the engulfing trigger), volume and indicators (confirmation), entries (break-and-confirm), stops (below support), targets/R:R (next resistance, ~2.3R), position sizing (1% → share count), and psychology (accepting the outcome). The whole course executes in one decision.'],
    ['What separates an A+ setup from a mediocre one?', 'The amount of confluence and the reward-to-risk. An A+ setup has multiple aligned reasons at one place, a clear trigger, a tight logical stop, and a target several R away. Mediocre setups have one reason, a vague trigger, or poor R:R — and are best skipped.'],
    ['How do I keep improving after going live?', 'Keep journaling and reviewing by setup, judge process over outcome, change one thing at a time, keep risk small, and periodically re-validate your edge. Improvement is incremental and data-driven, not a search for a new strategy after every losing streak.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Internalise the full loop (scan → analyse → plan → size → execute → manage → journal) until it is automatic, and only take setups with real confluence and ≥~2R, sized at a small fixed risk.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Executing the pieces in isolation, skipping sizing or journaling, judging by single outcomes, or scaling up before the process and discipline are proven.'],
    ['In real trading', 'Consistent, selective execution of a positive-expectancy process with disciplined risk — over hundreds of trades — is what swing-trading success actually looks like; no single trade defines it.']],
  src: ['Investopedia: Swing Trading — Full Overview', 'https://www.investopedia.com/terms/s/swingtrading.asp'] });
